"1. Pharm Biol. 2025 Dec;63(1):877-895. doi: 10.1080/13880209.2025.2586351. Epub \n2025 Nov 19.\n\nAn exploratory evaluation of the interaction risk between herbal products and \npharmaceutical medicines used concurrently for disease management in Blantyre, \nMalawi.\n\nNyirenda KK(1), Mponda J(1), Chikowe I(1), Kawonga E(2), Gomani Phiri NT(3), \nMsukwa M(4), Phiri C(5), Roe AL(6), Paine MF(7), Gurley B(8), Oketch-Rabah H(9), \nGafner S(10), Krzykwa J(11), Mitchell CA(11), Embry MR(11), Pettit S(11), Smith \nDJ(1).\n\nAuthor information:\n(1)Department of Pharmacy, Kamuzu University of Health Sciences, Blantyre, \nMalawi.\n(2)AO Alliance, Lilongwe, Malawi.\n(3)Partners in Health Malawi, Lilongwe, Malawi.\n(4)Equip Group, Lilongwe, Malawi.\n(5)Department of Medicine, Queen Elizabeth Central Hospital, Blantyre, Malawi.\n(6)Procter & Gamble Healthcare, Cincinnati, OH, USA.\n(7)Department of Pharmaceutical Sciences, College of Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical \nSciences, Washington State University, Spokane, WA, USA.\n(8)National Center for Natural Products Research, School of Pharmacy, University \nof Mississippi, University, MS, USA.\n(9)United States Pharmacopeia, Rockville, MD, USA.\n(10)American Botanical Council, Austin, TX, USA.\n(11)Health and Environmental Sciences Institute, Washington, DC, USA.\n\nCONTEXT: The use of herbal products in Malawi remains widespread and culturally \nsignificant, often occurring alongside pharmaceutical medicine treatments. As \nthe burden of non-communicable diseases such as diabetes and hypertension \ncontinues to rise, the potential for herbal-drug interactions (HDIs) represents \nan underexamined public health concern.\nOBJECTIVE: Evaluate the concurrent use of herbal products and pharmaceutical \nmedicines among patients with diabetes or hypertension in a large health care \nfacility in Malawi and identify potential adverse HDIs.\nMATERIALS & METHODS: An exploratory mixed-methods, cross-sectional study was \nconducted with 301 patients attending a diabetes or hypertension clinic at Queen \nElizabeth Central Hospital in Blantyre, Malawi. Participants self-reported \nherbal and pharmaceutical use, and a targeted literature review was undertaken \nto assess potential pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic interactions between \ncommonly reported herbal products and prescribed medications.\nRESULTS: Participants reported concurrent use of a wide variety of herbal \nproducts (e.g., garlic, ginger, okra, lemon, mango, moringa) with prescription \nmedicines (e.g., metformin, insulin, glibenclamide, hydrochlorothiazide, \nenalapril, amlodipine). While clinical outcomes were not independently verified, \nliterature review findings, in some cases, indicated meaningful potential for \nHDIs.\nDISCUSSION AND CONCLUSION: This study provides foundational data on \nherbal-pharmaceutical co-use in Malawi and highlights the need for expanded \nresearch, improved documentation of herbal use in healthcare settings, and \nimproved education for patients and providers. Integrating awareness of herbal \nproduct use into clinical care is essential, and the methodology and findings \nmay inform future hypothesis-driven studies across Africa and other regions \nwhere traditional and modern medicine use overlaps.\n\nDOI: 10.1080/13880209.2025.2586351\nPMCID: PMC12632214\nPMID: 41259156 [Indexed for MEDLINE]\n\nConflict of interest statement: No potential conflict of interest was reported \nby the author(s)."